<BaseLayout title="Summer at Christmas — Huanyu Chen"
  description="SIGGRAPH Asia 2023 in Sydney and a first SIGGRAPH talk at 9 a.m., a harbour ferry past the Opera House, and a side trip to Canberra, Australia's planned capital.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · December 2023</p>
  <h1>Summer at Christmas</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">SIGGRAPH Asia in Sydney, my first SIGGRAPH talk, a day on the harbour, and two days in Canberra.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>8 days · December 10–17, 2023</strong><br />
    Sydney (Darling Harbour, Taronga Zoo, the harbour ferry, SIGGRAPH Asia 2023) → Canberra (the zoo, ANU,
    the Parliamentary Triangle, the National Gallery) → Sydney (a night at the Opera House) → home<br />
    one conference · one first SIGGRAPH talk, at 9 a.m. · two cities · two zoos · one Opera House, photographed from every side ·
    sunsets after eight in the evening, a week before Christmas
  </div>

  <p>
    Every December, SIGGRAPH Asia, the Asian edition of the big computer graphics conference, lands in a different
    city of the Asia-Pacific. In 2023 it landed in Sydney. For a graphics student based in Los Angeles that meant two
    unusual things at once: a conference in a city I had only ever seen in pictures, and a week of high summer in the
    middle of December. The jacket stayed in the suitcase. The shopping centers played Christmas carols while people
    walked past in shorts and sunglasses, and the sun did not set until after eight.
  </p>
  <p>
    This was also, looking back, the first of a series. The following summer's post on this site begins at SIGGRAPH
    in Denver, and conferences have a way of turning into trips. As with that one, everything below is pieced together
    from the camera roll: where each photo was taken, and when. Where the photos are silent, I have kept quiet too.
    The shape of the week is simple. There are two days of Sydney before the conference got going, then the
    conference itself, then a side trip to Canberra, the capital, and one last night in Sydney at the Opera House.
  </p>

  <h2>Darling Harbour, the first evening</h2>
  <p>
    We landed on Sunday, December 10th, after the overnight flight across the Pacific, and the camera roll opens that
    evening at <strong>Darling Harbour</strong>. This was our
    neighborhood for the week, because the conference was held at the International Convention Centre at the bay's
    southern end. Just before seven, the sun was a pale disc in a sky full of thin, feathery cloud, sinking behind the
    towers of Pyrmont, and the old timber piers of Cockle Bay Wharf had gone dark against the glare.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-darling-harbour.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-darling-harbour.webp'} alt="Map of Darling Harbour with the first evening's walk from Cockle Bay across Pyrmont Bridge, the convention center and an evening view over the bay." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Darling Harbour: the first evening's walk, and the convention center at the south end of the bay.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cockle-bay-sun.webp'} alt="A hazy evening sun over Darling Harbour, with ferries moored below an old timber pier and a tall tower against the sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cockle Bay, a little before seven on the first evening.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Darling Harbour used to be the working end of Sydney. For more than a century it was wharves, warehouses and
    railway goods yards, and it was rebuilt as a waterfront of museums, restaurants and promenades in time for
    Australia's bicentenary in 1988. The ferries still come in. That evening, catamarans in blue and yellow livery
    were tied up along the wharf, and across the water the new skyline of Barangaroo stood out. The tallest tower,
    the twisting white one, is Crown Sydney, finished in 2020 and now the tallest building in the city.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cockle-bay-ferries.webp'} alt="Blue-and-yellow catamaran ferries moored at Cockle Bay Wharf under a clear evening sky, with the Barangaroo towers behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ferries at Cockle Bay Wharf, with Barangaroo behind.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We walked out onto <strong>Pyrmont Bridge</strong>, which crosses the bay on low iron trusses. It opened in 1902
    as one of the first electrically powered swing bridges in the world. Its central span still swings open to let
    tall ships through, though these days it carries only people. From the middle of it the whole basin opens up. On one side are the convention center and its glassy neighbors. Facing them is a new building whose
    curved glass shell bulges out over the expressway like a wave about to break, with an IMAX sign tucked under its
    chin. The water was dead flat, and it doubled everything.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'darling-harbour-curve.webp'} alt="A curved glass building and a tall tower reflected in the still water of Darling Harbour at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view south from Pyrmont Bridge.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pyrmont-bridge.webp'} alt="The wave-shaped glass building beside the elevated roadway, with the city towers beyond under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The wave-shaped building, with the city towers behind.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Down at the south end, the waterfront plaza was busy with families out in the long evening light. Children ran
    around a sunken spiral of stone steps, and nearby stood a giant pink lattice ball, which in December could only be
    read as a Christmas ornament. Behind it, hoardings promised "a reimagined waterfront" in front of a construction
    site, a reminder that this part of Sydney is still being rebuilt.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'darling-harbour-plaza.webp'} alt="Overhead view of a waterfront plaza with a sunken circle of steps, a large pink lattice sphere and people strolling." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Darling Harbour waterfront, Sunday evening.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>A day on the harbour</h2>
  <p>
    Monday was the one full free day before the conference, and we spent it on and around the water. The morning began
    across the harbour at <strong>Taronga Zoo</strong>, on the steep north shore in Mosman. The zoo moved to this site
    in 1916, and it may have the best view of any zoo in the world. "Taronga" is usually translated as "beautiful view"
    or "water view", and it is not false advertising.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-1211.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-1211.webp'} alt="Map of the route: Taronga Zoo and its koalas, lunch in Kirribilli, a ferry past the Opera House to Circular Quay, and George Street." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: Taronga Zoo, lunch in Kirribilli, and a ferry across to the city.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Giraffes with a skyline</h3>
  <p>
    The first animal of the day was a capybara, which had dragged itself into the shade of a tree beside a pile of
    fresh greens. It looked supremely unbothered, as capybaras always do. It was already warm at eleven in the
    morning, with the kind of dry, bright Australian heat that makes shade the most valuable thing in any enclosure.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'taronga-capybara.webp'} alt="A capybara resting in the shade beside a tree trunk, next to a pile of green fodder." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A capybara, in the shade.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then came the famous view. The giraffe enclosure sits on a terrace high above the water, and the zoo's planners
    put it where the giraffes have the city as a backdrop. Over their sandy yard, across a band of trees and a mile of
    blue harbour, stands the whole of downtown Sydney. You can pick out Sydney Tower, the white sails of the Opera House
    at the waterline, and the arch of the Harbour Bridge. The giraffes, a whole herd of them, stood around ignoring
    one of the most photographed views on Earth. Two of them wandered over to the fence and peered down at us with
    the polite, slightly superior interest that giraffes bring to everything.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'taronga-giraffe-skyline.webp'} alt="A giraffe at the edge of its enclosure, with the Sydney skyline and the Opera House across the harbour framed by trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sydney, as seen from the giraffes' yard.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'taronga-giraffes.webp'} alt="Two giraffes looking over a fence, with the Harbour Bridge, the Opera House and the city behind them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Two giraffes, the Harbour Bridge and the Opera House.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A little lower down the hill, past the zoo's own wharf, the trees open up and the view becomes the whole harbour.
    Sailboats were tacking across the water, a ferry was crossing, and on the far side the city ran in one unbroken
    line of towers from the grey ships of the naval base on the left to the Opera House on the right. The sky had
    those small, flat-bottomed fair-weather clouds that drift across Sydney in summer, and the water was the deep
    blue that only a harbour this size, open to the ocean, seems to manage.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'taronga-harbour.webp'} alt="Panorama of Sydney Harbour from Taronga: the city skyline across deep blue water, a sailboat, and the Opera House at right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The harbour from Taronga, late morning.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Before leaving we found the koalas, two of them, wedged into the forks of a eucalyptus tree in a planted enclosure
    near the top of the zoo. Koalas sleep for up to twenty hours a day, a habit usually blamed on their diet of
    eucalyptus leaves, which are tough, low in energy and mildly toxic. These two were keeping to schedule. Neither of
    them moved the whole time we were there.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'taronga-koalas.webp'} alt="Two koalas asleep in the forks of a eucalyptus tree in a planted enclosure." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Two koalas, keeping to schedule.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Lunch on the water in Kirribilli</h3>
  <p>
    Lunch was a few bays west in Kirribilli, at a small café perched so close to the water that the window seemed to
    open straight onto the harbour. The table was a narrow ledge under a sash window, the water a startling turquoise
    just beyond the glass, rocking gently with the wake of passing ferries. On the plates were a waffle piled with
    fruit, blueberries, edible flowers, toasted coconut and a scoop of ice cream, and a bowl of glazed fried chicken
    under a heap of rocket.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kirribilli-waffle.webp'} alt="A waffle topped with ice cream, fruit and edible flowers, with drinks and a salad bowl, against a soft blue harbour background." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lunch in Kirribilli.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kirribilli-window.webp'} alt="A small table under an open window, with two plates of food and turquoise harbour water directly outside." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A table with the harbour right outside the window.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The ferry past the Opera House</h3>
  <p>
    Then we took the ferry back across. From the wharf in Kirribilli you look east down the harbour, and that afternoon
    the view was of Garden Island, the Royal Australian Navy's main fleet base on the east coast. A big grey warship
    was tied up alongside its cranes, looking out of place among the sailboats.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'garden-island.webp'} alt="Across blue water, the naval base at Garden Island with a large grey warship and cranes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Garden Island, from the Kirribilli wharf.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The crossing to Circular Quay takes only a few minutes, but it passes the single most famous building in the
    southern hemisphere at close range. The ferry swung out from under the Harbour Bridge, and there was the
    <strong>Sydney Opera House</strong>, a cluster of white shells on its own point of land, close enough to see the
    pattern of the tiles. From the water the building makes sense in a way it never does in photographs. It is designed
    to be seen from all sides and from below, and a harbour ferry is exactly the right place to see it from.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'opera-house-ferry.webp'} alt="The Sydney Opera House seen side-on from a ferry, its white shells against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Opera House, side-on from the ferry.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'opera-house-rail.webp'} alt="The Opera House across the water beyond a ferry's railing and a sign telling passengers to remain seated." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Passengers must remain seated. Nobody did.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    For someone who works in computer graphics, the Opera House is also a story about geometry. The Danish architect
    Jørn Utzon won the design competition in 1957 with sketches of soaring free-form shells that nobody knew how to
    build. For years he and the engineers at Ove Arup tried parabolas and ellipsoids, and some of the earliest
    computers ever used in structural engineering ground through the calculations. Nothing worked until Utzon found
    what became known as the "spherical solution". Every shell is cut from the surface of the same sphere, so all
    of them share the same curvature and could be built from standard, prefabricated ribs. It is a beautiful piece
    of reasoning: a building that looks completely free is in fact made from one shape.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'opera-house-close.webp'} alt="The Opera House at close range from the water, its white tiled shells gleaming in bright sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Close enough to see the tiles.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The tiles are their own story. There are just over a million of them, made in Sweden in two finishes, glossy white
    and matte cream, and laid in a chevron pattern, so that the shells shine without blinding anyone. In the hard
    afternoon sun they glittered like snow. The building took sixteen years to finish, cost many times its budget, and
    lost its architect along the way: Utzon resigned in 1966 and never returned to see it completed. It opened in
    1973 and became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2007, one of the youngest buildings ever listed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Coming into Circular Quay, the ferry's bow, with its flags and its little mast, pointed straight at the city.
    A cruise ship the height of an apartment block was berthed at the Overseas Passenger Terminal, its white flank
    lined up against the glass towers behind.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'circular-quay-ferry.webp'} alt="From the bow of a green ferry flying flags, a large cruise ship berthed in front of the Sydney skyline." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coming into Circular Quay, with a cruise ship in port.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From the quay we walked up into the city. Since 2019 a long stretch of <strong>George Street</strong>, Sydney's
    oldest street, has been closed to cars and given over to the light rail and to people on foot. The result is a
    shady sandstone canyon of old banks and hotels, with trees and creepers spilling off the façades, café tables on
    the pavement, and a tram gliding silently down the middle every few minutes. It was half past two on a Monday, and
    half the city seemed to be out getting coffee.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'george-street.webp'} alt="Pedestrianised George Street in Sydney, with sandstone buildings draped in greenery, café umbrellas and light-rail tracks." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>George Street, given back to people on foot.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>SIGGRAPH Asia 2023</h2>
  <p>
    Then came the conference. SIGGRAPH Asia 2023 ran from December 12th to 15th at the <strong>International
    Convention Centre Sydney</strong>, the long glass-and-steel building at the south end of Darling Harbour, which
    opened in 2016. If you have been to the summer SIGGRAPH in North America, the Asian edition is the same
    conference in a more compact form. It has the technical papers, the courses, the exhibition, the art gallery and
    the computer animation festival, but it feels more like a large family gathering than a festival.
  </p>
  <p>
    For me it was also a debut. I was there to present a paper, <a href="/publications"><em>Capturing Animation-Ready
    Isotropic Materials Using Systematic Poking</em></a>, which had appeared in <em>ACM Transactions on Graphics</em>,
    and it was my first talk at any SIGGRAPH. The program, it is fair to say, did not roll out the red carpet. The talk
    was at nine in the morning, in the earliest paper session of the day, which is an hour when even the speakers are
    still looking for coffee. The session was also a slightly puzzling home for it. It was first announced as "All About
    Rendering", then quietly renamed "All About Animation", and the other papers in it were mostly about animating
    characters, while mine was about poking soft objects to measure what they are made of. A paper about squishy
    materials, in a session about character animation that used to be about rendering: it had, at least, a certain
    shape-shifting logic to it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The audience was what you would expect at nine o'clock on a conference morning: small, loyal, and
    caffeinated. I gave the talk anyway, with all the seriousness of someone who had rehearsed it far too many times,
    and it went fine. Every SIGGRAPH speaker has to give a first talk somewhere. Mine just happened to be at breakfast,
    on the other side of the world, in a session that could not decide what it was about.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rest of a conference day looks, in the camera roll, mostly like lunch. On Tuesday it was a spiced rice dish heaped in a
    white bowl, eaten near the venue. On Thursday the camera recorded the exhibition-hall menu in detail: a cup of
    coconut yoghurt layered with mango and passionfruit under a crust of granola, and a breakfast burger with bacon,
    cheese and a fried egg.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'conference-lunch.webp'} alt="A white dish heaped with a spiced rice dish, with a spoon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tuesday lunch.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'conference-burger.webp'} alt="A breakfast burger with a fried egg in a cardboard tray, beside a cup of yoghurt with passionfruit and granola." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Thursday: the convention-centre special.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On Wednesday evening, a little before half past six, the light over Darling Harbour was spectacular. The sky was
    packed with small puffy clouds, like a field of cotton, and below them the city towers, Sydney Tower and the curved
    glass building we had seen on the first night were lit gold by the low sun. Underneath, the marina was full of
    boats and the expressway was full of people heading home.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'darling-harbour-evening.webp'} alt="Darling Harbour in the early evening: towers, Sydney Tower and a curved glass building under a sky full of small clouds, with a marina below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Darling Harbour, Wednesday evening.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The exhibition floor</h3>
  <p>
    Thursday morning was spent on the exhibition floor. One wall was given over to recruitment: board after board of
    printed job ads, each under the conference logo and a studio's name. Weta FX, the visual effects company from
    Wellington behind <em>The Lord of the Rings</em> and <em>Avatar</em>, was hiring technical directors and riggers.
    Industrial Light &amp; Magic and an art and design school had boards of their own. Every ad ended in a QR code
    and a booth number. For a room full of students, it was the most-read wall in the building.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'job-boards.webp'} alt="A wooden wall covered with printed job recruitment notices from several visual effects studios." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The recruitment wall.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wetafx-board.webp'} alt="A SIGGRAPH Asia 2023 job recruitment notice from Weta FX for a technical director position." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Weta FX, hiring.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The booths had the same mix of art and industry you find at every SIGGRAPH. One was hung with film posters from
    shows and films its artists had worked on: <em>Willow</em>, <em>Mission: Impossible</em>, <em>Obi-Wan Kenobi</em>
    and <em>Andor</em>, under a slogan about "the legacy of iconic storytelling". Another was a tall yellow banner for
    <strong>Animal Logic</strong>, the Sydney studio that made <em>Happy Feet</em>, <em>The LEGO Movie</em> and
    <em>Peter Rabbit</em>. Its characters were all lined up on the banner: penguins, owls, a LEGO construction worker and
    a pair of rabbits. It felt right to find them here. For a lot of graphics people, Animal Logic is a large
    part of the reason Sydney is on the map at all.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'film-posters.webp'} alt="An exhibition booth with posters for Willow, Mission: Impossible, Obi-Wan Kenobi and Andor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A booth hung with film posters.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'animal-logic.webp'} alt="A yellow Animal Logic banner showing characters from the studio's animated films, labelled Sydney, LA, Vancouver." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Animal Logic: Sydney, LA, Vancouver.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Lunch that day was the most Australian meal of the week: fish and chips in a cardboard tray, with calamari rings,
    a wedge of lemon, and, less traditionally, a can of milk bubble tea.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'fish-and-chips.webp'} alt="A cardboard tray of fish and chips with calamari rings and a lemon wedge, next to a can of milk bubble tea." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fish and chips, conference edition.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    That evening we went for a walk a couple of kilometers south, toward the University of Sydney. In a park off
    Cleveland Street, under tall old trees, stood a carved wooden pole topped by a bird with outstretched wings,
    catching the very last of the sun at seven o'clock. The lawns had gone gold and the shadows stretched halfway across
    the park. It was a quiet end to a loud day.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'totem-pole.webp'} alt="A tall carved wooden pole topped by a bird with outstretched wings, in a park at golden hour." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A carved pole in a park off Cleveland Street, Thursday evening.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Canberra</h2>
  <p>
    On Friday we went to <strong>Canberra</strong>, about 280 kilometers southwest of Sydney, by coach, if memory
    serves. Canberra is a capital
    that was invented on purpose. When the Australian colonies federated in 1901, neither Sydney nor Melbourne would
    accept the other as the capital, so the constitution required a new city to be built in New South Wales, at least a
    hundred miles from Sydney. An international design competition in 1912 was won by two architects from Chicago,
    Walter Burley Griffin and Marion Mahony Griffin. Their plan is still the city's skeleton: grand axes lined up on
    the surrounding hills, and an artificial lake in the middle.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-1215.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-1215.webp'} alt="Map of Canberra: the National Zoo and Aquarium beside the lake, then the Australian National University." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 6: the National Zoo and Aquarium in the afternoon, ANU in the evening.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>The National Zoo and Aquarium</h3>
  <p>
    The first stop was the <strong>National Zoo &amp; Aquarium</strong>, at the western end of Lake Burley Griffin
    beside the dam that holds the lake in. It is a smaller, more intimate zoo than Taronga, and in places you get
    remarkably close to the animals. In a dim indoor exhibit, a reticulated python lay looped across a sandy floor
    under artificial vines. Reticulated pythons are generally counted as the longest snakes in the world, and they
    can reach more than six meters.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canberra-python.webp'} alt="A reticulated python lying in loops on the sandy floor of a dim rocky enclosure." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A reticulated python, Canberra.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The leopards were asleep, flat out on a wooden deck in the afternoon heat, and the only thing between them and us
    was a pane of glass. Up close a sleeping leopard is a lesson in pattern. The rosettes get smaller and denser toward
    the head and legs, and the whole coat looks as though it was procedurally generated with a great deal of care.
    Next door, the capuchin monkeys had a tall mesh house of ropes and platforms, with a view out to the trees.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canberra-leopard.webp'} alt="A leopard asleep on its side on a wooden deck, seen through glass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A leopard, asleep against the glass.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'canberra-capuchins.webp'} alt="A tall mesh enclosure with ropes, platforms and plants for capuchin monkeys." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The capuchins' house.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Near the picnic lawn, under a shade roof, lay the biggest snake of the day. It was a python the size of a sofa,
    coiled on the bare earth with its head raised. It was also, we are fairly confident, a sculpture, which is the
    only reason anybody was standing that close.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'python-sculpture.webp'} alt="A giant python sculpture coiled under a shade roof near a picnic lawn." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The largest python in Canberra.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>An evening at ANU</h3>
  <p>
    Around six we walked across the campus of the <strong>Australian National University</strong>, which spreads
    along the lake's northern shore at the foot of Black Mountain. ANU was founded in 1946 as a research university for
    the new nation, and it is still the only university established by Australia's federal parliament. The campus is
    green and spread-out, with gum trees everywhere. One new building had a façade of white hexagons, each framing a
    window, like a honeycomb tipped on its side. Out on a lawn, three giant white letters spelled out the university's
    name for anyone who needed a photo.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'anu-honeycomb.webp'} alt="A modern building at ANU with a façade of white interlocking hexagonal window frames." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A honeycomb façade at ANU.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'anu-letters.webp'} alt="Giant white letters spelling ANU on a lawn, with trees behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Just in case you forgot where you were.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The Parliamentary Triangle</h3>
  <p>
    Saturday morning was the reason for coming to Canberra. At half past nine, under a sky without a single cloud, we
    were on the lawns in front of <strong>Parliament House</strong>. The building opened in 1988, Australia's
    bicentennial year, and its architects, Mitchell/Giurgola &amp; Thorp, did something unusual with it. Rather than
    put the parliament on top of Capital Hill, they dug it into the hill, so the building sits inside the landform with
    grass sweeping up and over its roof. The idea was that the people should, quite literally, stand above their
    representatives.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-1216.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-1216.webp'} alt="Map of the walk through Canberra's Parliamentary Triangle: Parliament House, Old Parliament House, Questacon, the lake shore, the National Gallery sculpture garden and James Turrell's skyspace, then the Acton Peninsula." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 7: down the land axis on foot, from Parliament House to the lake, then the National Gallery.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Above it all stands the flagpole: four stainless-steel legs leaning together into a single mast, eighty-one meters
    tall, flying a flag roughly the size of the side of a double-decker bus. From the far end of the lawn the whole
    building almost vanishes into the hill, and what is left is a white colonnade, a long green slope and that
    enormous steel tripod against the blue.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'parliament-house-wide.webp'} alt="A wide green lawn leading up to Parliament House, which is set low into the hill beneath its giant flagpole." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Parliament House, set into Capital Hill.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'parliament-house.webp'} alt="The white colonnaded front of Parliament House under its four-legged steel flagpole and the Australian flag." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The flagpole: eighty-one meters of stainless steel.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A red sign nearby carried an etched map of the whole district, and its shape explains the city in one glance. The
    circle at the top is Capital Hill, ringed by its road. The long triangle below it is the
    <strong>Parliamentary Triangle</strong>, the wedge of land running down to the lake. The Griffins designed it to
    hold the institutions of the nation: parliament, the courts, the library and the gallery. The shape is a
    keyhole.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'triangle-keyhole.webp'} alt="An etched map in the shape of a keyhole on a red sign: a circle above a long triangle of streets and buildings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Parliamentary Triangle, in one shape.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Looking north from Parliament House, the land axis runs straight down the slope to <strong>Old Parliament
    House</strong>, a long, low white building that served as the "provisional" parliament from 1927 until the new
    one opened in 1988, a provisional period of sixty-one years. It is now the Museum of Australian Democracy. Directly
    behind it, exactly on the axis, rises the dark cone of Mount Ainslie. That alignment is not a coincidence. The
    Griffins laid out the whole city by sighting from hilltop to hilltop.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'old-parliament-house.webp'} alt="Old Parliament House, a long white building across green lawns, with the rounded peak of Mount Ainslie directly behind it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Old Parliament House, with Mount Ainslie on the axis behind.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Walking down toward the lake we passed <strong>Questacon</strong>, the national science centre. Its building was
    a joint gift from Australia and Japan for the 1988 bicentenary. Outside the entrance stands a cut-steel portrait of
    Albert Einstein, all hair and moustache, on a block stamped with the most famous equation in physics. It is the
    kind of sculpture that only works for one person, and for him it works perfectly.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'questacon-einstein.webp'} alt="A cut-metal sculpture of Albert Einstein's face on a block inscribed E = mc², in front of a white tiled wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Einstein, outside Questacon.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At the bottom of the slope, the land axis meets <strong>Lake Burley Griffin</strong>. The lake was finished in
    1964 by damming the Molonglo River, half a century after the Griffins drew it. From the shore you can see the
    whole second half of the axis at once: across the water, up the long red avenue of Anzac Parade, to the copper
    dome of the Australian War Memorial at the foot of Mount Ainslie. A tourist boat cut across the lake. For a capital city, Canberra on a Saturday morning is astonishingly quiet.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'land-axis.webp'} alt="View across Lake Burley Griffin along Canberra's land axis to the Australian War Memorial and Mount Ainslie, with a boat on the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The land axis across the lake to Mount Ainslie.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The National Gallery, and a room for the sky</h3>
  <p>
    The last stop on the triangle was the <strong>National Gallery of Australia</strong> and its sculpture garden,
    which wanders through native bush along the lakeshore. Above the road by the gallery, a mirrored sphere hung from
    cables high in the air, catching the sun. In a clearing stood George Baldessin's <em>Pears</em>, a group of giant
    bronze pears that look as if they had rolled off a still life and kept growing.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nga-sphere.webp'} alt="A mirrored sphere suspended on cables high above a tree-lined road, against a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A sphere hung over the road by the gallery.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nga-pears.webp'} alt="Giant bronze pear sculptures on a circular base in a grassy clearing surrounded by reeds and eucalyptus." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>George Baldessin's <em>Pears</em>.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And then there was the piece I had most wanted to see: James Turrell's <em>Within without</em>, opened in 2010.
    From the outside it is just a grassy mound in a pond. You walk down a ramp into it and find yourself in a
    square, open-roofed chamber with tall, sloping walls the color of red earth, framing a hard triangle of sky. In the
    middle, surrounded by a pool of brilliant turquoise water, stands a domed stupa of dark basalt. Inside the stupa,
    which we could only look at from outside, is a round room with an opening in the roof. Turrell has spent his career
    making rooms whose only subject is the light of the sky. Even from the courtyard the effect is uncanny. The walls
    are so plain and the pool so blue that the sky above looks less like sky and more like a flat panel of color
    someone has painted on.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'turrell-within-without.webp'} alt="A domed stone stupa rising from a turquoise pool inside a red-walled chamber open to a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>James Turrell, <em>Within without</em>.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We finished the morning on the <strong>Acton Peninsula</strong>, next to the National Museum, at a café terrace
    right on the lake. The order was a strawberry milkshake under a mountain of whipped cream and red syrup, an iced
    coffee, and brunch for two, with the lake and the far shore glittering in the sun. Then we left Canberra.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'acton-milkshake.webp'} alt="A strawberry milkshake topped with whipped cream and an iced coffee on a café table overlooking Lake Burley Griffin." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lakeside, the Acton Peninsula.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Back to Sydney: a night at the Opera House</h2>
  <p>
    We came back by train, an earlier one than we had booked, and by evening we were
    in Sydney with one last plan for the trip: a show at the <strong>Opera House</strong>,
    <em>Gatsby at the Green Light</em>, at seven o'clock in the Studio.
    Having seen it from a giraffe's enclosure and from a ferry, it seemed only fair to go inside. When we came back
    out onto the forecourt at about half past eight, the sun had just gone down behind the city, and the whole of
    Circular Quay was out on the promenade.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-finale.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-finale.webp'} alt="Map of the last evening and morning in Sydney: the Opera House forecourt, the view across Sydney Cove, and brunch at Darling Square." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 7–8: a night at the Opera House, and a last brunch in Darling Square.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Looking west, the Harbour Bridge was lit up against a grey-violet sky, its arch strung with lights. At its far end,
    below the bridge, glowed the fairground lights of <strong>Luna Park</strong>, the 1935 amusement park whose giant
    grinning face has been greeting the harbour for nearly ninety years. The bridge itself opened in 1932, and
    Sydneysiders have called it "the Coathanger" for almost as long. Looking the other way, the sky over the city had
    gone gold and pink, the towers had started to light up, and the old globe lamps along the promenade had come on.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'harbour-bridge-night.webp'} alt="The Sydney Harbour Bridge lit at dusk, with the lights of Luna Park glowing beneath it on the far shore and a globe lamp in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Harbour Bridge and Luna Park at dusk.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'circular-quay-dusk.webp'} alt="Sunset over the Sydney skyline seen from the Opera House forecourt, with lamps glowing along the waterfront." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset over the city, from the Opera House forecourt.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Later, from across Sydney Cove at The Rocks, we looked back at it fully lit. At night the Opera House stops being a
    building and becomes a lantern. The shells are floodlit from below, so they glow soft white against a black sky,
    while the glass walls under them show the warm light of the foyers, and the whole thing is doubled in the moving
    water. A boat went past just as the picture was taken and blurred into a red streak across the foreground.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'opera-house-night.webp'} alt="The Sydney Opera House floodlit at night, reflected in dark water, with a blurred boat passing in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Opera House at night, from across Sydney Cove.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>A last brunch</h3>
  <p>
    Sunday morning, the last of the trip, ended where the week had started, near Darling Harbour, with brunch in
    <strong>Darling Square</strong>. The square is a newly built neighborhood whose centerpiece is The Exchange,
    a round building by the Japanese architect Kengo Kuma, wrapped from top to bottom in a spiral of timber battens.
    Brunch was squid-ink spaghetti with clams, flying-fish roe and white wood-ear fungus, and a mushroom risotto with a
    soft burrata, roasted tomatoes and a crown of fried enoki. It was, by some distance, the most photogenic food of the
    week.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'darling-square-pasta.webp'} alt="Squid-ink spaghetti with clams, orange fish roe and white fungus in a grey bowl." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Squid-ink spaghetti with clams.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'darling-square-risotto.webp'} alt="A mushroom risotto with burrata, roasted tomatoes, edamame and crispy fried enoki mushrooms." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Risotto, burrata and fried enoki.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The camera roll stops a little before noon. After that, all that remained was the long flight home, back across the
    Pacific into the northern winter.
  </p>

  <h2>Looking back</h2>
  <p>
    It was a short trip, and most of it was spent at a conference, including one nine o'clock talk that I will
    always remember more fondly than its audience will. But conferences are good at putting you in places
    you would never otherwise have gone, and this one did it twice. Sydney is the obvious half of the story: the
    harbour, the bridge, the shells. Even so, the camera roll is full of things I would not have predicted. There are
    giraffes posing in front of a skyline, a café with the sea outside the window, and a capybara that would not get up.
  </p>
  <p>
    Canberra was the surprise. A city designed from scratch can easily feel like a diagram, and at times Canberra does.
    But it is a diagram made with real care. Hills sight onto hills, a lake was made to sit between them, and the
    parliament was buried in a hill so that people could walk over it. And at the bottom of it all, in a red room in a
    grassy mound, there is a pool of turquoise water under an open square of sky. For someone who spends his days
    thinking about how shapes and light are computed, a city built on lines of sight and a building cut from a single
    sphere made a fitting pair. The summer SIGGRAPH was next, in Denver, and that trip turned into something else
    entirely.
  </p>
